# Text files are stored and checked out with LF everywhere.
#
# This is not cosmetic. The raw records in results/raw/, the aggregate CSV and the
# calibrations are evidence: their sha256 values are quoted in results/README.md and in
# docs/FINDINGS.md, and they are compared against the byte counts `report` regenerates.
# Git's default autocrlf behaviour on Windows would hand a cloner CRLF copies of them,
# which would look like tampering. Forcing LF keeps a clone byte-identical to the machine
# the runs happened on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats are never touched by line-ending conversion.
*.png binary
*.zip binary
*.ipynb binary

# Three files were written on Windows and their CRLF line endings are part of the exact
# bytes whose length and sha256 are quoted in results/README.md and docs/FINDINGS.md.
# They are pinned to CRLF so a clone on any platform reproduces them byte-for-byte rather
# than silently rewriting them.
results/benchmark_results.csv text eol=crlf
results/profiling/profile-smoke-cab8be82e4cd-profiling.json text eol=crlf
results/raw/slice-cpu-smoke-8b0f61630f43.jsonl text eol=crlf
